 First, we can expand the determinant along the first row. Using the properties of determinants, we get
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\ b & c & a \\ c & a & b \end{vmatrix} &= a \begin{vmatrix} c & a \\ a & b \end{vmatrix} - b \begin{vmatrix} b & a \\ c & b \end{vmatrix} + c \begin{vmatrix} b & c \\ c & a \end{vmatrix}\\
&= a(cb - a^2) - b(b^2 - ac) + c(bc - c^2)\\
&= 3abc - a^3 - b^3 - c^3
\end{align*}
Next, we can use the fact that $a,$ $b,$ $c$ are the roots of $x^3 + px + q = 0$ to express $a^3,$ $b^3,$ and $c^3$ in terms of $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$
\begin{align*}
a^3 &= -p a - q\\
b^3 &= -p b - q\\
c^3 &= -p c - q
\end{align*}
Substituting these expressions into the determinant, we get
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\ b & c & a \\ c & a & b \end{vmatrix} &= 3abc - (-p a - q - p b - q - p c - q)\\
&= 3abc + p(a + b + c) + 3q\\
&= 3abc + p \cdot 0 + 3q\\
&= 3q
\end{align*}
Therefore, the determinant is $\boxed{3q}.$ </think> <answer> 3q </answer>

